### What lock does a static synchronized method use?

A static synchronized method locks on the Class object. There is only one Class object per class in the JVM, so this lock is shared by all instances and all threads.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class AccountService {
    // Locks on AccountService.class
    public static synchronized void globalAudit() {
        System.out.println("Audit running on " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
        try { Thread.sleep(2000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
    }

    // Equivalent explicit form
    public static void globalAuditExplicit() {
        synchronized (AccountService.class) {
            System.out.println("Audit running");
        }
    }
}
```

### What lock does an instance synchronized method use?

An instance synchronized method locks on `this`, the specific object instance.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountService {
    private BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");

    // Locks on 'this'
    public synchronized void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
    }

    // Also locks on 'this': same lock as debit()
    public synchronized BigDecimal getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
```

### Can static and instance synchronized run at the same time?

Yes. They use different lock objects, so they do not block each other.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ConcurrentLockDemo {
    private BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");

    public static synchronized void globalAudit() {
        // Locks on ConcurrentLockDemo.class
        System.out.println("Audit on " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
        try { Thread.sleep(2000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
    }

    public synchronized void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        // Locks on 'this'
        balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        System.out.println("Debit on " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ConcurrentLockDemo service = new ConcurrentLockDemo();

        // These two run in PARALLEL because they use different locks
        new Thread(ConcurrentLockDemo::globalAudit, "AuditThread").start();
        new Thread(() -> service.debit(new BigDecimal("5000")), "DebitThread").start();
    }
}
```

```text
Audit on AuditThread
Debit on DebitThread
```

Both threads run simultaneously because `globalAudit()` locks on `ConcurrentLockDemo.class` while `debit()` locks on the `service` instance.

### What is the complete lock combination matrix?

| Combination | Lock objects | Blocks? |
|---|---|---|
| static sync + static sync (same class) | Same Class lock | Yes |
| instance sync + instance sync (same object) | Same `this` | Yes |
| instance sync + instance sync (different objects) | Different `this` | No (parallel) |
| static sync + instance sync | Class vs `this` | No (parallel) |

### What if both methods need to be mutually exclusive?

If the audit must prevent debits during its execution, both methods must use the same lock object.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class SharedLockService {
    private static final Object SHARED_LOCK = new Object();
    private BigDecimal balance = new BigDecimal("100000.00");

    public static void globalAudit() {
        synchronized (SHARED_LOCK) {
            System.out.println("Audit: no debits can happen now");
        }
    }

    public void debit(BigDecimal amount) {
        synchronized (SHARED_LOCK) {
            balance = balance.subtract(amount);
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Developers often assume that all synchronized methods on an object block each other. This is only true for instance methods on the same instance. A static synchronized method uses a completely different lock.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Class lock contention**: a static synchronized method blocks ALL threads across ALL instances. If `globalAudit()` takes 10 seconds, no thread in the entire JVM can call any other static synchronized method on that class.
- **Prefer instance-level locking**: static synchronization is a global bottleneck. Use it only when the operation truly needs JVM-wide exclusion.
- **ReadWriteLock for audits**: if the audit only reads data, use a `ReadWriteLock`. Multiple audit threads can run concurrently (read lock), and debits take the write lock for exclusive access.
